Thinking: We need to answer user question: "How do I load a .pmtiles file as a source in MapLibre GL JS?" Provide step-by-step instructions, code examples, mention installing pmtiles library, registering protocol, using url, handling CORS, raster vs vector, example with map initialization, maybe React notes. Use skill content. Provide thorough answer.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **.pmtiles** archive (vector, raster, or raster‑DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles protocol library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` URL scheme. The tiny **pmtiles** npm package adds a protocol handler that makes range‑request fetching work.

```bash
# From your project root
npm install pmtiles
# or, if you use Yarn / pnpm
yarn add pmtiles
# pnpm add pmtiles
```

> **Why a library?**  
> The handler reads the archive’s header, builds a TileJSON on‑the‑fly, and serves each tile via HTTP Range requests. Without it you would have to write a custom source that knows the PMTiles layout.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (or any module that runs at app start)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol object – it holds an internal cache of opened archives
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Tell MapLibre to use it for the "pmtiles" scheme
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// Optional – expose a cleanup function if you ever need to remove it
export const removePmtilesProtocol = () => maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Tip:** In a React/Vue/Svelte app, import this module in the top‑level entry point (e.g. `index.js` or `App.jsx`) so the handler is installed before the first `<Map>` component mounts.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### 3.1 Vector tiles (most common)

```js
// map-setup.js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import './pmtiles-setup';   // <-- makes sure the protocol is registered

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // ONE source that points at the whole .pmtiles file
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL can be:
        //   • "pmtiles://https://my.cdn.com/tiles/world.pmtiles"
        //   • "pmtiles://s3://my-bucket/world.pmtiles" (if you use an S3‑compatible host)
        //   • plain "https://…" if you prefer to register the protocol for the https scheme
        url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/data/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',   // exact name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': [
            'interpolate',
            ['linear'],
            ['zoom'],
            5, 0.5,
            12, 2,
            18, 8
          ]
        }
      }

      // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own source‑layer name
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Key points**

| What | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`) | The PMTiles protocol reads the archive header and automatically supplies `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, `bounds`, etc. If you use `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` MapLibre will assume a default `maxzoom: 22` and may request zoom levels that don’t exist, resulting in blank tiles. |
| Keep **one source** per archive | A PMTiles file can contain many vector layers. All of them are accessed through the same source; you differentiate them with `"source-layer"` in each style layer. |
| `source-layer` names | Run `pmtiles show <file>` (CLI) or inspect the TileJSON to see the exact layer names. They are case‑sensitive. |

---

### 3.2 Raster tiles (imagery)

```js
{
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'myPmtiles',
  'source-layer': 'sat',   // optional for raster; you can omit it
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.9 }
}
```

The source definition stays the same (`type: 'raster'` instead of `'vector'`). The archive must contain raster tiles (PNG/JPEG/WebP). No `source-layer` is required, but you can keep it if the archive stores multiple raster layers.

---

### 3.3 Raster‑DEM (terrain)

```js
// Add the DEM source
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/elevation/world-dem.pmtiles',
  // The encoding must match how the DEM was generated.
  // Common values: "terrarium" (Google‑style) or "mapbox" (Mapbox‑style)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  tileSize: 256   // optional, defaults to 256
});

// Enable 3‑D terrain in the style
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

Now you can add hillshade or 3‑D extrusion layers that reference the DEM.

---

## 4️⃣ CORS & HTTP Range support on the host

| Requirement | How to satisfy |
|-------------|----------------|
| **Range requests** (`Accept‑Ranges: bytes`) | All major static hosts (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel) support this out of the box. |
| **CORS** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`) | Set the header to `*` or to your domain. In S3 you can add a bucket CORS rule: <br>`<CORSRule><AllowedOrigin>*</AllowedOrigin><AllowedMethod>GET</AllowedMethod><AllowedHeader>*</AllowedHeader></CORSRule>` |
| **Cache‑Control** (optional but recommended) | `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable` if the file never changes. This lets browsers and CDNs cache the whole archive and each range response. |

If you host on a CDN (CloudFront, Cloudflare, etc.) make sure the CDN forwards the `Range` header to the origin; otherwise the CDN will return the whole file and defeat the purpose.

