## TL;DR  
MapLibre GL JS v4 changed the **protocol‑handler API**:

| v3 (old) | v4 (new) |
|----------|----------|
| `addProtocol(name, (params, callback) => { … })` | `addProtocol(name, (params, abortSignal) => Promise<{data, cacheControl?, expires?, headers?}> )` |
| You called `callback(err, data, cacheControl, expires)` | You **return** a promise that resolves to an object containing the tile bytes (and optional HTTP‑like metadata). |
| No built‑in abort support | The third argument is an **AbortSignal** you can pass to `fetch` (or any async work) so the request can be cancelled when the map discards the tile. |

Because of that the callback you wrote is never called any more, so the map never receives tile data.  
Fix it by **rewriting the handler** to return a promise (or an async function) that resolves to the same shape of data.

Below is a complete “before → after” migration guide, plus a ready‑to‑copy example for a custom vector‑tile source (the same pattern works for raster, raster‑dem, or any binary tile format).

---

## 1. What changed in MapLibre v4

### 1.1 Signature

```js
// v3
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  // params: { url, ... }
  // callback(err, data, cacheControl?, expires?)
});

// v4
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abortSignal) => {
  // must **return** a Promise that resolves to:
  // { data: ArrayBuffer, cacheControl?: string, expires?: string, headers?: object }
});
```

* `abortSignal` is an `AbortSignal` (the same object you would pass to `fetch` as `{signal: abortSignal}`).
* The handler **must not** call a callback; it must **return** a promise (or be an `async` function).

### 1.2 Return shape

```ts
interface ProtocolResponse {
  data: ArrayBuffer;               // required – the raw tile bytes
  cacheControl?: string;           // optional – e.g. "max-age=3600"
  expires?: string;                // optional – RFC‑1123 date string
  headers?: Record<string,string>; // optional – any extra headers you want MapLibre to see
}
```

If you only need the tile bytes, just return `{ data }`.  
If you want the map to respect caching, include `cacheControl` or `expires`.

### 1.3 Abort handling

When the map no longer needs a tile (e.g. the user pans away), it will call `abortSignal.abort()`.  
If you forward that signal to `fetch` (or any other async work) the request is cancelled cleanly and the map won’t wait for a stale response.

---

## 2. Migration checklist

| ✅ What to do | 🔧 How |
|--------------|--------|
| **Update the handler signature** | Change `(params, callback)` → `(params, abortSignal)` and **return** a promise. |
| **Replace `callback(err, data…)`** | Resolve the promise with `{ data }`. Throw/reject on error. |
| **Pass the abort signal to your request** | `fetch(url, { signal: abortSignal })` (or `axios.CancelToken`, etc.). |
| **Remove any `callback`‑related code** | No more `callback(null, …)`; just `return { data }`. |
| **(Optional) Add caching metadata** | Return `cacheControl` or `expires` if you want the browser/CDN to cache the tile. |
| **Register the protocol before any map is created** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('my', myHandler);` (usually at app start‑up). |
| **If you used a third‑party library (e.g. `pmtiles`)** | The library already ships a v4‑compatible `Protocol` class – just keep using it. |
| **Test with the devtools network tab** | You should see a request to `my://…` that returns a `200` with an `arraybuffer` payload. |

---

## 3. Full working example (custom vector tiles)

Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that works with **MapLibre GL JS v4**.  
It serves tiles from a remote HTTP endpoint, but you could replace the `fetch` with any custom logic (e.g. reading from IndexedDB, generating on‑the‑fly, etc.).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 – custom protocol example</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Register a custom protocol named "my"
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const myProtocolHandler = async (params, abortSignal) => {
    // `params.url` is the full URL after the protocol, e.g.
    //   my://https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
    // Strip the leading "my://" to get the real HTTP URL.
    const httpUrl = params.url.replace(/^my:\/\//, '');

    // Use the abortSignal so the request can be cancelled.
    const response = await fetch(httpUrl, { signal: abortSignal });

    if (!response.ok) {
      // Throwing rejects the promise → MapLibre treats it as a tile error.
      throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status}`);
    }

    // Vector tiles are binary (PBF).  Get them as an ArrayBuffer.
    const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

    // OPTIONAL: expose caching headers to MapLibre (helps the browser cache).
    const cacheControl = response.headers.get('Cache-Control') || undefined;
    const expires      = response.headers.get('Expires')      || undefined;

    // Return the shape MapLibre expects.
    return {
      data: arrayBuffer,
      cacheControl,
      expires,
      // You can also forward any other header you think is useful:
      // headers: { 'x-custom': 'value' }
    };
  };

  // Register the protocol **once**, before any map instance is created.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('my', myProtocolHandler);

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Create the map, referencing the custom protocol in a source
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // The source points at the custom protocol.
        // Note the use of `url:` (not `tiles:`) – MapLibre will ask the
        // protocol for a TileJSON, which the handler above satisfies
        // automatically because we return the raw tile bytes.
        custom: {
          type: 'vector',
          // The URL can be absolute or relative; the protocol handler will
          // receive the whole string after "my://".
          url: 'my://https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'custom',
          'source-layer': 'water',   // <-- name of the layer inside the PBF
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        // add more layers that reference the same source but different
        // source‑layer names (roads, buildings, labels, …)
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Clean‑up (optional but recommended)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // If you ever destroy the map and never create another one,
  // remove the protocol to avoid memory leaks.
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('my');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What changed compared to a v3 implementation?

| v3 code snippet | v4 replacement |
|-----------------|----------------|
| ```js<br>maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {<br>  fetch(url).then(r=>r.arrayBuffer()).then(buf=>callback(null, buf));<br>});``` | ```js<br>maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortSignal) => {<br>  const resp = await fetch(url, {signal: abortSignal});<br>  const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();<br>  return {data: buf};<br>});``` |

*No more `callback(null, …)` – you **return** the object.*  
*The second argument is now an `AbortSignal` instead of a callback.*  

